From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:39:56 +0200
Subject: COutputStreamBuffer: write the overflow char, not its int's first
 byte

overflow() wrote the character that didn't fit into the full buffer with
PHYSFS_write(m_file, &ch, 1, 1), ch being the streambuf's int_type: the
first byte of an int is the character on little-endian machines and 0 on
big-endian ones. So on the PS3 every file Colobot wrote through it got a
NUL for the 513th byte (and the one after each further 512): colobot.ini
read "ShadowM\0ppingResolution=1024", and saved games and the player's
programs would be cut the same way. Now the character itself is written.

Forwarded: not-needed (to offer upstream: a plain big-endian bug)

diff --git a/src/common/resources/outputstreambuffer.cpp b/src/common/resources/outputstreambuffer.cpp
--- a/src/common/resources/outputstreambuffer.cpp
+++ b/src/common/resources/outputstreambuffer.cpp
@@ -86,7 +86,9 @@ std::streambuf::int_type COutputStreamBuffer::overflow(std::streambuf::int_type
     // write final char
     if (ch != traits_type::eof())
     {
-        bytes_written = PHYSFS_write(m_file, &ch, 1, 1);
+        // the char itself, not the first byte of the int_type holding it (0 on big-endian)
+        const char c = traits_type::to_char_type(ch);
+        bytes_written = PHYSFS_write(m_file, &c, 1, 1);
         if (bytes_written <= 0)
             return traits_type::eof();
     }
